  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Wednesday...\n\nWinds become light again tonight, which will allow areas of fog \nto develop again, especially along river channels. After sunrise \nFriday morning, fog will begin to dissipate, eroding by early \nafternoon. The same scenario is likely (40-60% chance) Friday \nnight into Saturday morning. \n\nOtherwise, Friday and Saturday will be mild and mostly dry. There\nis a chance (20-50%) for rain and snow showers, primarily over \nthe mountains, as PWATs increase to 150-200% of normal and weak \nshortwaves in the flow provide a little energy. Currently, only \nlight showers are expected, with a 30% chance of up to 0.10\" of \nrain and SWE over the mountains.\n\nSunday into Monday, a low pressure system is expected to move \nonshore and through the region. Uncertainty remains in the exact\ntrack and timing of this system. However, there is higher \nconfidence in conditions becoming breezy Sunday night into Monday \nas a cold front associated with the system moves through and in \nsnow accumulation being limited to the mountains and immediate \nfoothills. Widespread gusts out of the northwest of 20 to 40 mph\nare expected into Monday afternoon. For the plains, the current \nbest chance (50-70%) for at least 0.50\" of rain is from Billings \neast. During the overnight hours, particularly Monday night, a\nlittle snow may mix in over the higher hills of, but the chance \nof any accumulating snow is less than 20%. Over the mountains, \nnorth facing slopes will be favored for snow. The Bighorns have \nthe best chance for snow, with a 70% chance of at least 6\" through\nMonday night. Over the Absaroka/Beartooth mountains, the chance \nfor the same is 50%, and over the Crazy mountains, the chance is \n<20%. Those planning to recreate in the mountains should be\nprepared for wintry conditions.\n\nBehind the system exiting late Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday look\nto remain mostly dry. A weak cold front late Tuesday into early\nWednesday may bring an isolated shower or two (<20% chance).\nArcher",
